2.explanation:
1. components/index.js – This file enumerates and registers all language components. To introduce Apex support it must be updated to include an entry for "prism-apex.js" so that the build system and the Prism core know about the new component.

2. components/prism-apex.js – The new language definition/alias file. It should contain the code

   ;(function(Prism) {
     Prism.languages.apex = Prism.languages.java;
   })(Prism);

   Without it, Prism has no knowledge of an "apex" language and cannot apply Java syntax highlighting to Apex code.

3. components/prism-apex.min.js – The minified counterpart of prism-apex.js. It’s required if you ship the minified bundle or if your autoloader/plugin system loads the ".min.js" versions in production.

4. components.js – This root-level file usually aggregates metadata about all components (their paths, dependencies, labels, etc.). It will need an entry for "apex" mapping to the newly added prism-apex.js, so tools like the autoloader or the custom download page can include Apex.

5. dependencies.js – If your build or loader tracks language dependencies (e.g., that Apex depends on clike → java), you should add an entry for "apex" pointing at "java" here. That will ensure that when someone requests Apex highlighting, the Java component is loaded first.

By creating the alias file and registering it in the index and build metadata, Prism will successfully load Apex as an alias of Java and highlight Apex code correctly.
